   "date": "2016-03-26T17:22:07Z",
   "subject": "Re: Underdispersion of negative binomial model (0) with Nmix.gof.test (AICcmodavg)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"times new roman, serif\">Hi Rob,</font><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><br></font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\">I don&#39;t quite follow the reduction from 162 to 23 (is 162 the total # visits? Not sure how this is being fit), but I might stick with the Poisson mixture.   G-o-f indicates the Poisson mixture barely over-dispersed. A very low value of c-hat may also indicate lack of fit, and I think that maybe this is the case with the NB here. A</font><span style=\"font-family:&#39;times new roman&#39;,serif\">s a note, it is generally best to estimate c-hat using a full model to ensure that the model set includes a suitable candidate.  Using  G-o-f  assessments of  a null model to compare mixture distributions is problematic: if there is any real ecological variance, the NB null model should generally perform far better than the Poisson null model.</span></div><div><span style=\"font-family:&#39;times new roman&#39;,serif\"><br></span></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\">The AHM book (chapter 6) has a useful section on issues with the Negative Binomial mixture often providing better &#39;fit&#39; or goodness of fit, and poorer prediction, and there have been several papers that have discussed NB vs. ZIP, e.g., Joseph et al. 2009 <font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">Modeling abundance using </span><em style=\"outline:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px;background-image:initial;background-size:initial;background-origin:initial;background-clip:initial;background-position:initial;background-repeat:initial\">N</em><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">-mixture models: the importance of considering ecological mechanisms , in Ecologial Applications.</span></font></font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\"><br></span></font></font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">Cheers,</span></font></font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\"><br></span></font></font></div><div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">John</span></font></font></div></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "y2OPZsXxIwAJ",
   "from": "Kery Marc",
   "author": "00ebeb6e0521112d",
   "date": "2016-03-26T18:32:23Z",
   "subject": "RE: [unmarked] Re: Underdispersion of negative binomial model (0) with Nmix.gof.test (AICcmodavg)",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n\r\n<div>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\">\r\n<font size=\"2\">Dear Rob,<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI agree with John and would use the Poisson mixture, which in terms of AIC is barely worse than the NB and seems to have hardly any overdispersion. I believe that at worst, you will underestimate abundance when using a Poisson where in fact you should use something\r\n allowing for more overdispersion. Usually, people would be OK with having a more &#39;conservative&#39; assessment of the &#39;health&#39; of a population, i.e., knowing that there are probably at least as many animals as the Poisson Nmix says.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI know that all this may seem a little unsatisfactory. I would use the NB if you really have (in terms of GoF) but ONLY when the resulting abundance estimates pass the commonsense GoF test, i.e., do not predict something outragously high.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAs we said in the AHM book, we think that more research is needed here to better understand how the NB model can sometimes be vastly preferred by GoF and yet predict unrealistic abundance levels.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nBest regards  --  Marc<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n<br>\r\n</font>\r\n<div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\">\r\n<hr>\r\n<div style=\"direction:ltr\"><font face=\"Tahoma\" color=\"#000000\" size=\"2\"><b>From:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">unma...@googlegroups.com</a> [<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">unma...@googlegroups.com</a>] on behalf of John Clare [<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">john....@maine.edu</a>]<br>\r\n<b>Sent:</b> 26 March 2016 18:22<br>\r\n<b>To:</b> unmarked<br>\r\n<b>Cc:</b> <a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">rj...@kent.ac.uk</a><br>\r\n<b>Subject:</b> [unmarked] Re: Underdispersion of negative binomial model (0) with Nmix.gof.test (AICcmodavg)<br>\r\n</font><br>\r\n</div>\r\n<div></div>\r\n<div></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     3,
     "<div><div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\"><div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\"><div>\r\n<div dir=\"ltr\"><font face=\"times new roman, serif\">Hi Rob,</font>\r\n<div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><br>\r\n</font></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\">I don&#39;t quite follow the reduction from 162 to 23 (is 162 the total # visits? Not sure how this is being fit), but I might stick with the Poisson mixture.   G-o-f indicates the Poisson mixture barely over-dispersed.\r\n A very low value of c-hat may also indicate lack of fit, and I think that maybe this is the case with the NB here. A</font><span style=\"font-family:&#39;times new roman&#39;,serif\">s a note, it is generally best to estimate c-hat using a full model to ensure that\r\n the model set includes a suitable candidate.  Using  G-o-f  assessments of  a null model to compare mixture distributions is problematic: if there is any real ecological variance, the NB null model should generally perform far better than the Poisson null\r\n model.</span></div>\r\n<div><span style=\"font-family:&#39;times new roman&#39;,serif\"><br>\r\n</span></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\">The AHM book (chapter 6) has a useful section on issues with the Negative Binomial mixture often providing better &#39;fit&#39; or goodness of fit, and poorer prediction, and there have been several papers that have discussed\r\n NB vs. ZIP, e.g., Joseph et al. 2009 <font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">Modeling abundance using </span><em style=\"outline:0px;vertical-align:baseline;color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">N</em><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">-mixture\r\n models: the importance of considering ecological mechanisms , in Ecologial Applications.</span></font></font></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\"><br>\r\n</span></font></font></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">Cheers,</span></font></font></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\"><br>\r\n</span></font></font></div>\r\n<div><font face=\"times new roman, serif\"><font size=\"2\"><span style=\"color:rgb(0,0,0);line-height:14px\">John</span></font></font></div>\r\n</div>\r\n<p></p></div></div></div></div>"
    ],
    [
     1,
     "<div><div style=\"direction:ltr;font-family:Verdana;color:#000000;font-size:10pt\"><div style=\"font-family:Times New Roman;color:#000000;font-size:16px\"><div>\r\n-- <br>\r\nYou received this message because you are subscribed to the Google Groups &quot;unmarked&quot; group.<br>\r\nTo unsubscribe from this group and stop receiving emails from it, send an email to\r\n<a href data-email-masked rel=\"nofollow\">unmarked+u...@googlegroups.com</a>.<br>\r\nFor more options, visit <a href=\"https://groups.google.com/d/optout\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"nofollow\" data-saferedirecturl=\"https://www.google.com/url?hl=en-US&amp;q=https://groups.google.com/d/optout&amp;source=gmail&amp;ust=1792452559633000&amp;usg=AOvVaw3d_Nl3NHz8l5i2dBS9ncg0\">\r\nhttps://groups.google.com/d/optout</a>.<br>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n</div>\r\n\r\n"
